{"content":{"attachment":{"filename":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/tagseq2tagseq/paper/sections/06_results.tex","snippet":"1\t\\section{Results}\n2\t\\label{sec:results}\n3\t\n4\t% Numbers in this section are generated from provenance/ledger.yaml via\n5\t% scripts/gen_values_tex.py and cited with value macros, so each traces to the run and\n6\t% code state that produced it; see provenance/README.md. (Author note, not rendered.)\n7\t\n8\t\\subsection{Cross-document attention beats scoring without the target}\n9\t\\label{sec:results:crossdoc}\n10\tOn the Wikipedia HotpotQA cross-document benchmark, the \\crossdoc model (bfs) attains a\n11\tcross-document negative log-likelihood of \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.nll_crossdoc}\n12\tagainst \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.nll_flat} for the same answers scored flat, over\n13\t$n=\\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.n}$ fired query--target pairs: a reduction of\n14\t$\\Delta\\mathrm{nll}=\\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.delta_nll}$ nats per token. The effect\n15\tcarries over to code, where dependencies are more local and the margins accordingly\n16\tsmaller: on Java RepoBench cross-document completion, cross-document attention lowers\n17\tNLL under every traversal --- from \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.random.delta_nll}\n18\t(random packing) to \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.rw.delta_nll} (random walk) --- and\n19\tPython RepoBench cross-doc shows\n20\t$\\Delta\\mathrm{nll}=\\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.delta_nll}$\n21\t($n=\\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.n}$). \\Cref{tab:traversal} gives every arm.\n22\t\n23\t\\begin{table}[t]\n24\t  \\centering\n25\t  \\small\n26\t  \\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrrr@{}}\n27\t    \\toprule\n28\t    Benchmark & Traversal & nll$_\\text{cross}$ & nll$_\\text{flat}$ & $\\Delta$nll & $n$ \\\\\n29\t    \\midrule\n30\t    Wiki HotpotQA & bfs         & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.n} \\\\\n31\t    Wiki HotpotQA & dfs         & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.dfs.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.dfs.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.dfs.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.dfs.n} \\\\\n32\t    Wiki HotpotQA & random walk & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.rw.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.rw.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.rw.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.rw.n} \\\\\n33\t    Wiki HotpotQA & random      & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.random.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.random.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.random.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.random.n} \\\\\n34\t    \\midrule\n35\t    Java RepoBench & bfs         & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.n} \\\\\n36\t    Java RepoBench & dfs         & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.dfs.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.dfs.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.dfs.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.dfs.n} \\\\\n37\t    Java RepoBench & random walk & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.rw.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.rw.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.rw.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.rw.n} \\\\\n38\t    Java RepoBench & random      & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.random.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.random.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.random.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.random.n} \\\\\n39\t    \\midrule\n40\t    Python RepoBench & bfs      & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.n} \\\\\n41\t    \\bottomrule\n42\t  \\end{tabular}\n43\t  \\caption{Paired cross-doc vs.\\ flat mean NLL on the fired subset, per \\crossdoc\n44\t  training arm. Wikipedia arms: \\texttt{wiki\\_merged}, $\\mu_\\text{lr}=0.003$,\n45\t  $\\mu_\\text{wd}=0.3$, VE off; the bfs arm was scored on the full fired set\n46\t  ($n=738$) and the other three on the $\\texttt{max\\_grants}=256$ re-evaluation subset\n47\t  ($n=377$), so absolute NLLs are comparable within but not across those two groups.\n48\t  Java arms: final checkpoints of the Java sweep; Python: \\texttt{max\\_grants}=256\n49\t  re-evaluation of the \\texttt{thestack} \\crossdoc arm.}\n50\t  \\label{tab:traversal}\n51\t\\end{table}\n52\t\n53\t\\subsection{The cross-document gain is specific, not memorization}\n54\tTwo controls confirm the reduction above comes from reading the \\emph{correct} linked\n55\tdocument, not from generic extra context or verbatim re-exposure of overlapping text.\n56\tFirst, a derangement \\emph{placebo}: for each query we keep the original link identifiers\n57\t(so the grant still fires) but replace the granted document's content with another\n58\texample's, so the model attends to a wrong-but-plausible document. This collapses most of\n59\tthe benefit, leaving a placebo separation (placebo minus cross-document NLL) of\n60\t\\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.placebo_sep} (95\\% CI\n61\t\\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.placebo_sep.ci}) on HotpotQA, and\n62\t\\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.placebo_sep}\n63\t(\\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.placebo_sep.ci}) and\n64\t\\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.placebo_sep}\n65\t(\\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.placebo_sep.ci}) on Python and Java RepoBench --- all\n66\tCIs excluding zero. Second, we stratify the cross-document $\\Delta$nll by the\n67\ttarget--context $n$-gram overlap $\\alpha$ (RETRO's leakage protocol): even in the\n68\tlowest-overlap stratum ($\\alpha<0.05$), where the target cannot be copied from the granted\n69\tcontext, the reduction persists at\n70\t\\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.leakage_lowalpha.delta_nll}\n71\t(\\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.leakage_lowalpha.delta_nll.ci},\n72\t$n=\\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.leakage_lowalpha.n}$) on HotpotQA,\n73\t\\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.leakage_lowalpha.delta_nll}\n74\t(\\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.leakage_lowalpha.delta_nll.ci},\n75\t$n=\\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.leakage_lowalpha.n}$) on Python, and\n76\t\\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.leakage_lowalpha.delta_nll}\n77\t(\\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.leakage_lowalpha.delta_nll.ci},\n78\t$n=\\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.leakage_lowalpha.n}$) on Java --- again all CIs\n79\texcluding zero. The gain therefore reflects cross-document reasoning over the right link,\n80\tnot surface duplication.\n81\t\n82\t\\subsection{Single-document benchmarks are unaffected}\n83\t\\label{sec:results:singledoc}\n84\tCross-document attention has nothing to attend to on an isolated document, and the\n85\tcontrols confirm it does not change the base model (\\Cref{tab:singledoc-tuned}): at the\n86\ttuned recipe the three recipe-matched masks are indistinguishable on isolated-document\n87\theld-out perplexity and on the two benchmarks these models clear chance on, HellaSwag\n88\tand ARC-easy, with overlapping $95\\%$ intervals. The full four-mask panel (HellaSwag,\n89\tARC-easy, PIQA, WinoGrande, OpenBookQA, BoolQ) on an earlier, undertrained matched set\n90\ttells the same story at chance-level accuracy (\\Cref{tab:singledoc-4mask},\n91\t\\Cref{app:results}). The claim is that the mask does not \\emph{degrade}\n92\tsingle-document capability, not a strong-capability equivalence: these are small models\n93\ttrained for a few billion tokens.\n94\t\n95\t\\begin{table}[t]\n96\t  \\centering\n97\t  \\small\n98\t  \\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrr@{}}\n99\t    \\toprule\n100\t    & \\doccausal & \\crossdoc & \\concatlink \\\\\n101\t    \\midrule\n102\t    Held-out ppl $\\downarrow$ & \\val{singledoc_tuned.held_out_ppl.doc_causal} & \\val{singledoc_tuned.held_out_ppl.cross_doc_link} & \\val{singledoc_tuned.held_out_ppl.doc_concat_link} \\\\\n103\t    HellaSwag & \\val{singledoc_tuned.hellaswag.doc_causal} \\val{singledoc_tuned.hellaswag.doc_causal.ci} & \\val{singledoc_tuned.hellaswag.cross_doc_link} \\val{singledoc_tuned.hellaswag.cross_doc_link.ci} & \\val{singledoc_tuned.hellaswag.doc_concat_link} \\val{singledoc_tuned.hellaswag.doc_concat_link.ci} \\\\\n104\t    ARC-easy  & \\val{singledoc_tuned.arc_easy.doc_causal} \\val{singledoc_tuned.arc_easy.doc_causal.ci} & \\val{singledoc_tuned.arc_easy.cross_doc_link} \\val{singledoc_tuned.arc_easy.cross_doc_link.ci} & \\val{singledoc_tuned.arc_easy.doc_concat_link} \\val{singledoc_tuned.arc_easy.doc_concat_link.ci} \\\\\n105\t    \\bottomrule\n106\t  \\end{tabular}\n\n... [235 lines truncated] ...","type":"edited_text_file"},"injection_type":"attachment/edited_text_file"},"context":{"branch":"paper-prose-draft","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/tagseq2tagseq","head":"66ae5bc942464f47e6398e0ac64d929a2aa9663f","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/tagseq2tagseq.git"},"id":"ev2-ed67831c3a5978d81be08467fba92141556ddcaca1862023bc672847fd49b510","kind":"context_injection","meta":{"actor":{"type":"system"},"session_id":"5cbbe416-90e8-4a21-93e7-044acf862880","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.252"},"occurred_at":"2026-09-01T17:15:11.636Z","producer":{"tool":"cledger","version":"0.25.0"},"raw":{"data":{"attachment":{"filename":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/tagseq2tagseq/paper/sections/06_results.tex","snippet":"1\t\\section{Results}\n2\t\\label{sec:results}\n3\t\n4\t% Numbers in this section are generated from provenance/ledger.yaml via\n5\t% scripts/gen_values_tex.py and cited with value macros, so each traces to the run and\n6\t% code state that produced it; see provenance/README.md. (Author note, not rendered.)\n7\t\n8\t\\subsection{Cross-document attention beats scoring without the target}\n9\t\\label{sec:results:crossdoc}\n10\tOn the Wikipedia HotpotQA cross-document benchmark, the \\crossdoc model (bfs) attains a\n11\tcross-document negative log-likelihood of \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.nll_crossdoc}\n12\tagainst \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.nll_flat} for the same answers scored flat, over\n13\t$n=\\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.n}$ fired query--target pairs: a reduction of\n14\t$\\Delta\\mathrm{nll}=\\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.delta_nll}$ nats per token. The effect\n15\tcarries over to code, where dependencies are more local and the margins accordingly\n16\tsmaller: on Java RepoBench cross-document completion, cross-document attention lowers\n17\tNLL under every traversal --- from \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.random.delta_nll}\n18\t(random packing) to \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.rw.delta_nll} (random walk) --- and\n19\tPython RepoBench cross-doc shows\n20\t$\\Delta\\mathrm{nll}=\\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.delta_nll}$\n21\t($n=\\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.n}$). \\Cref{tab:traversal} gives every arm.\n22\t\n23\t\\begin{table}[t]\n24\t  \\centering\n25\t  \\small\n26\t  \\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrrr@{}}\n27\t    \\toprule\n28\t    Benchmark & Traversal & nll$_\\text{cross}$ & nll$_\\text{flat}$ & $\\Delta$nll & $n$ \\\\\n29\t    \\midrule\n30\t    Wiki HotpotQA & bfs         & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.n} \\\\\n31\t    Wiki HotpotQA & dfs         & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.dfs.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.dfs.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.dfs.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.dfs.n} \\\\\n32\t    Wiki HotpotQA & random walk & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.rw.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.rw.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.rw.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.rw.n} \\\\\n33\t    Wiki HotpotQA & random      & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.random.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.random.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.random.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.random.n} \\\\\n34\t    \\midrule\n35\t    Java RepoBench & bfs         & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.n} \\\\\n36\t    Java RepoBench & dfs         & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.dfs.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.dfs.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.dfs.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.dfs.n} \\\\\n37\t    Java RepoBench & random walk & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.rw.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.rw.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.rw.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.rw.n} \\\\\n38\t    Java RepoBench & random      & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.random.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.random.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.random.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.random.n} \\\\\n39\t    \\midrule\n40\t    Python RepoBench & bfs      & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.nll_crossdoc} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.nll_flat} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.delta_nll} & \\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.n} \\\\\n41\t    \\bottomrule\n42\t  \\end{tabular}\n43\t  \\caption{Paired cross-doc vs.\\ flat mean NLL on the fired subset, per \\crossdoc\n44\t  training arm. Wikipedia arms: \\texttt{wiki\\_merged}, $\\mu_\\text{lr}=0.003$,\n45\t  $\\mu_\\text{wd}=0.3$, VE off; the bfs arm was scored on the full fired set\n46\t  ($n=738$) and the other three on the $\\texttt{max\\_grants}=256$ re-evaluation subset\n47\t  ($n=377$), so absolute NLLs are comparable within but not across those two groups.\n48\t  Java arms: final checkpoints of the Java sweep; Python: \\texttt{max\\_grants}=256\n49\t  re-evaluation of the \\texttt{thestack} \\crossdoc arm.}\n50\t  \\label{tab:traversal}\n51\t\\end{table}\n52\t\n53\t\\subsection{The cross-document gain is specific, not memorization}\n54\tTwo controls confirm the reduction above comes from reading the \\emph{correct} linked\n55\tdocument, not from generic extra context or verbatim re-exposure of overlapping text.\n56\tFirst, a derangement \\emph{placebo}: for each query we keep the original link identifiers\n57\t(so the grant still fires) but replace the granted document's content with another\n58\texample's, so the model attends to a wrong-but-plausible document. This collapses most of\n59\tthe benefit, leaving a placebo separation (placebo minus cross-document NLL) of\n60\t\\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.placebo_sep} (95\\% CI\n61\t\\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.placebo_sep.ci}) on HotpotQA, and\n62\t\\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.placebo_sep}\n63\t(\\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.placebo_sep.ci}) and\n64\t\\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.placebo_sep}\n65\t(\\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.placebo_sep.ci}) on Python and Java RepoBench --- all\n66\tCIs excluding zero. Second, we stratify the cross-document $\\Delta$nll by the\n67\ttarget--context $n$-gram overlap $\\alpha$ (RETRO's leakage protocol): even in the\n68\tlowest-overlap stratum ($\\alpha<0.05$), where the target cannot be copied from the granted\n69\tcontext, the reduction persists at\n70\t\\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.leakage_lowalpha.delta_nll}\n71\t(\\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.leakage_lowalpha.delta_nll.ci},\n72\t$n=\\val{crossdoc.wiki_hotpotqa.leakage_lowalpha.n}$) on HotpotQA,\n73\t\\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.leakage_lowalpha.delta_nll}\n74\t(\\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.leakage_lowalpha.delta_nll.ci},\n75\t$n=\\val{crossdoc.repobench_python.leakage_lowalpha.n}$) on Python, and\n76\t\\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.leakage_lowalpha.delta_nll}\n77\t(\\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.leakage_lowalpha.delta_nll.ci},\n78\t$n=\\val{crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.leakage_lowalpha.n}$) on Java --- again all CIs\n79\texcluding zero. The gain therefore reflects cross-document reasoning over the right link,\n80\tnot surface duplication.\n81\t\n82\t\\subsection{Single-document benchmarks are unaffected}\n83\t\\label{sec:results:singledoc}\n84\tCross-document attention has nothing to attend to on an isolated document, and the\n85\tcontrols confirm it does not change the base model (\\Cref{tab:singledoc-tuned}): at the\n86\ttuned recipe the three recipe-matched masks are indistinguishable on isolated-document\n87\theld-out perplexity and on the two benchmarks these models clear chance on, HellaSwag\n88\tand ARC-easy, with overlapping $95\\%$ intervals. The full four-mask panel (HellaSwag,\n89\tARC-easy, PIQA, WinoGrande, OpenBookQA, BoolQ) on an earlier, undertrained matched set\n90\ttells the same story at chance-level accuracy (\\Cref{tab:singledoc-4mask},\n91\t\\Cref{app:results}). The claim is that the mask does not \\emph{degrade}\n92\tsingle-document capability, not a strong-capability equivalence: these are small models\n93\ttrained for a few billion tokens.\n94\t\n95\t\\begin{table}[t]\n96\t  \\centering\n97\t  \\small\n98\t  \\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrr@{}}\n99\t    \\toprule\n100\t    & \\doccausal & \\crossdoc & \\concatlink \\\\\n101\t    \\midrule\n102\t    Held-out ppl $\\downarrow$ & \\val{singledoc_tuned.held_out_ppl.doc_causal} & \\val{singledoc_tuned.held_out_ppl.cross_doc_link} & \\val{singledoc_tuned.held_out_ppl.doc_concat_link} \\\\\n103\t    HellaSwag & \\val{singledoc_tuned.hellaswag.doc_causal} \\val{singledoc_tuned.hellaswag.doc_causal.ci} & \\val{singledoc_tuned.hellaswag.cross_doc_link} \\val{singledoc_tuned.hellaswag.cross_doc_link.ci} & \\val{singledoc_tuned.hellaswag.doc_concat_link} \\val{singledoc_tuned.hellaswag.doc_concat_link.ci} \\\\\n104\t    ARC-easy  & \\val{singledoc_tuned.arc_easy.doc_causal} \\val{singledoc_tuned.arc_easy.doc_causal.ci} & \\val{singledoc_tuned.arc_easy.cross_doc_link} \\val{singledoc_tuned.arc_easy.cross_doc_link.ci} & \\val{singledoc_tuned.arc_easy.doc_concat_link} \\val{singledoc_tuned.arc_easy.doc_concat_link.ci} \\\\\n105\t    \\bottomrule\n106\t  \\end{tabular}\n\n... [235 lines truncated] ...","type":"edited_text_file"},"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evint