{"content":{"blocks":[{"caller":{"type":"direct"},"id":"toolu_01U3G7PKzRFevGyeTx5jE1qF","input":{"file_path":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/tagseq2tagseq/paper/sections/06_results.tex","new_string":"\\subsection{The cross-document benefit scales with realized link density}\n\\label{sec:results:density}\\label{sec:sparsity}\nWith corpus, packing, and model held fixed and only the fraction $\\kappa$ of resolved\ngrants varied, the community-pack $\\Delta\\mathrm{nll}$ on every code graph rises\nmonotonically with $\\kappa$ at evaluation time, near-linearly\n(\\Cref{fig:sparsity-eval}, panel A), and is pinned to zero at\n$\\kappa=0$ (the \\doccausal identity; the instrument was verified to reproduce\n$\\Delta=0$ exactly on real checkpoints). Which edges are kept does not matter, only how\nmany: subsampling by edge and by node give the same lines. Pooling the nine code graphs\nand regressing $\\Delta\\mathrm{nll}$ on the number of grants the mask actually realizes\nper pack (out-degree $\\times$ co-packing rate $\\times\\kappa$) yields a single\ncross-dataset line with correlation $r=\\val{sparsity.crossdoc_law.eval_r_code}$ over\n\\fillin{$n$} points and slope \\fillin{nats per realized grant}\n(\\Cref{fig:sparsity-eval}, panel B; pooled regression detail in\n\\Cref{app:sparsity-regression}), extrapolating above today's densest corpus. Raw graph\nout-degree is the wrong axis, with correlation \\fillin{$r$, out-degree}: it counts\nedges whose targets are never co-packed and hence never attended.\n\nRetraining at each $\\kappa$ and re-evaluating at full density gives the train-time\nline (\\Cref{fig:sparsity-train}), which is monotone but strongly \\emph{concave}: most\nof the benefit is already present when the model trained on a quarter of the edges,\nand the train-time curve lies above the eval-time curve at low $\\kappa$ (training on a\nsparse graph beats evaluating a dense-trained model at that sparsity). The\ntwo-dimensional train-$\\kappa\\times$eval-$\\kappa$ grid (\\Cref{fig:sparsity-grid})\nmakes the asymmetry explicit: the dominant gradient is horizontal --- moving along the\neval-density axis swings $\\Delta$ by \\fillin{eval-axis swing} while moving along the\ntrain-density axis moves it by only \\fillin{train-axis swing}. The limiting case is\nthe $\\kappa_\\text{train}=0$ row --- a \\doccausal-trained model evaluated under the\n\\crossdoc mask --- which on TypeScript recovers $\\Delta\\mathrm{nll}=$\n\\fillin{dc-trained TS} against \\fillin{cdl-trained TS} for the model trained with\ngrants. On code, then, the cross-document benefit is overwhelmingly an\n\\emph{inference-time} effect: what the mask exposes at evaluation matters far more than\nwhether the model saw grants during training. The instrument itself is validated by a\ntraversal-time spot-check --- really removing edges from the graph before packing, so\nco-packing changes too, lands on the same dose--response curve at matched effective\ndensity (\\Cref{app:sparsity-traversal}). The Wikipedia graph is excluded from this\nanalysis pending an audit of the community-pack instrument on that corpus\n(\\Cref{sec:analysis}).\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n  \\centering\n  \\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{fig_sparsity.png}\n  \\caption{Eval-time graph-sparsity law. Panel A: $\\Delta$nll vs kept link fraction\n  per dataset (blue = code, orange = text; band = seeds 0--2). Panel B: cross-dataset\n  law --- $\\Delta$nll at full density vs the effective co-packed grant density the mask\n  actually saw.}\n  \\label{fig:sparsity-eval}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n  \\centering\n  \\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{fig_traintime.png}\n  \\caption{Train-time graph-sparsity law: models retrained at each link density\n  (solid) overlaid on the eval-time curve (dashed) for the same datasets.}\n  \\label{fig:sparsity-train}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n  \\centering\n  \\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{fig_grid2d.png}\n  \\caption{Cross-doc benefit $\\