{"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"An 11-source merged model's cross-document $\\Delta$ is \\fillin{ratio $\\times$}\n\\fillin{larger/smaller?} than single-domain specialists (flat scaling\n\\fillin{params} $\\to$ \\fillin{params}), suggesting joint training over many link types\n\\fillin{strengthens/weakens?} the cross-document machinery itself.\n% DO NOT ground yet: numbers are recomputable from results/merged_v2_{3p9b,8b}_ports_useline/*.json\n% (merge delta_real) + eval/benchmark_harness/reports/matrix/*.json (specialist baselines),\n% BUT the entire merged_all_v2 family was retracted 2026-08-14 (sequential-not-interleaved\n% dataloader bug; see RESULTS_graph_sparsity.md) and this claim is built on it. Kotlin/TS\n% baselines are external ports (indicative, not exact). Re-run on a fixed merge before grounding.\n% Add the per-port merge-vs-specialist table here once re-run.\n\n\\subsection{Density-aware scheduling efficiency}\n<<<<<<< HEAD\n\\label{sec:results:efficiency}\nPrecomputing packs and scheduling by density buys wall-clock time twice: link\ndetection leaves the training step, and no rank stalls at the all-reduce waiting for a\ndenser pack. Measured on a two-node, two-GPUs-per-node run on The Stack at $32$k\ncontext (\\Cref{tab:efficiency}), mean step time drops from\n\\val{efficiency.step_time.live_mean}\\,s to \\val{efficiency.step_time.precomputed_mean}\\,s\n(a \\val{efficiency.speedup_mean}$\\times$ speedup) and worst-case step time from\n\\val{efficiency.step_time.live_max}\\,s to \\val{efficiency.step_time.precomputed_max}\\,s\n(\\val{efficiency.speedup_max}$\\times$). The live path's step-time distribution is broad\nand structureless (coefficient of variation \\val{efficiency.cov.live}\\%), while under\ndensity bucketing the within-bucket variation collapses to a mean of\n\\val{efficiency.cov.precomputed_within_bucket}\\% --- the remaining spread is\n\\emph{between} buckets, deterministic and density-driven (\\Cref{fig:density-timing}).\nThe rank-stall component is most visible across nodes and near zero on a single\nNVLink node.\n% Grounding status: these figures are ledger LITERALS (efficiency.*) copied from\n% README.md \"Measured impact\"; the step_timing_rank*.csv files are not located in-repo\n% and carry no run_id. Tracked as grounding debt; replace with a kind:artifact source\n% once the originating run (or a re-timing at the same layout) lands. See TODOS.md.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n  \\centering\n  \\small\n  \\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrr@{}}\n    \\toprule\n    & Live & Precomputed, density-bucketed & Speedup \\\\\n    \\midrule\n    Mean step time & \\val{efficiency.step_time.live_mean}\\,s & \\val{efficiency.step_time.precomputed_mean}\\,s & \\val{efficiency.speedup_mean}$\\times$ \\\\\n    Max step time  & \\val{efficiency.step_time.live_max}\\,s  & \\val{efficiency.step_time.precomputed_max}\\,s  & \\val{efficiency.speedup_max}$\\times$ \\\\\n    Step-time CoV  & \\val{efficiency.cov.live}\\%             & \\val{efficiency.cov.precomputed_within_bucket}\\% within-bucket & \\\\\n    \\bottomrule\n  \\end{tabular}\n  \\caption{Wall-clock effect of density-aware scheduling ($2$ nodes $\\times$ $2$ GPUs,\n  The Stack, $32$k context). ``Live'' traverses the graph, detects links, and builds\n  the mask online with random pack-to-rank assignment; ``precomputed'' uses offline\n  packs and draws every rank's pack from the same density bucket at each step.}\n  \\label{tab:efficiency}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n  \\centering\n  \\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{density_aware_timing.png}\n  \\caption{Per-step wall-clock time over a training run. Top: live training with online\n  link detection and random pack density --- step times vary widely with no structure.\n  Bottom: precomputed, density-bucketed training, steps coloured by density bucket ---\n  sparse buckets are fast, dense buckets slow, and the pattern is deterministic because\n  every rank draws from the same bucket at every step.}\n  \\label{fig:density-timing}\n\\end{figure}\n=======\nDensity-aware batching yields a \\fillin{mean $\\times$} mean / \\fillin{max $\\times$} max\nstep-time \\fillin{speedup/slowdown?}, with per-bucket coefficient-of-variation\n\\fillin{$\\downarrow$/$\\uparrow$?}.\n% Wall-clock timing (step_timing_rank*.csv), not the eval harness. Raw CSVs are not located\n% in-repo and carry no run_id; only README.md prose + docs/images/density_aware_timing*.png\n% survive. Indicative only until the originating run + its CSVs are identified.\n\n\\subsection{Graph-sparsity scaling law}\n\\label{sec:sparsity}\nWe interpolate the fraction of resolved links a model sees, from $0\\%$ (\\doccausal)\nto $100\\%$ (\\crossdoc), by seeded per-edge subsampling of the grant map, and measure\nthe within-model cross-doc benefit $\\Delta\\mathrm{nll} =\n\\mathrm{nll}(\\doccausal)-\\mathrm{nll}(\\crossdoc)$ on each dataset's\n\\texttt{val\\_community} split. We do this both at eval time (fix a trained model, vary\nthe links it attends to; \\cref{fig:sparsity-eval}) and at train time (retrain on the\nreduced graph; \\cref{fig:sparsity-train}), and cross the two axes into a surface\n(\\cref{fig:sparsity-grid}).\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n  \\centering\n  \\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{fig_sparsity.png}\n  \\caption{Eval-time graph-sparsity law. Panel A: $\\Delta$nll vs kept link fraction\n  per dataset (blue = code, orange = text; band = seeds 0--2). Panel B: cross-dataset\n  law --- $\\Delta$nll at full density vs the effective co-packed grant density the mask\n  actually saw.}\n  \\label{fig:sparsity-eval}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n  \\centering\n  \\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{fig_traintime.png}\n  \\caption{Train-time graph-sparsity law: models retrained at each link density\n  (solid) overlaid on the eval-time curve (dashed) for the same datasets.}\n  \\label{fig:sparsity-train}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n  \\centering\n  \\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{fig_grid2d.png}\n  \\caption{Cross-doc benefit $\\Delta$nll over the full (train keep $\\times$ eval keep)\n  grid, per dataset. Rows = training-graph density, columns = inference-graph density;\n  keep${=}0$ column is the \\doccausal identity ($\\Delta{=}0$).}\n  \\label{fig:sparsity-grid}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n  \\item \\textbf{\\cref{fig:sparsity-eval} (eval-time lines + cross-dataset law).} How a\n  fixed trained model's cross-doc benefit grows as it is allowed to attend to more of\n  the graph's links. \\emph{Meaning:} for code the benefit rises near-linearly with\n  effective link density and the cross-dataset fit is strong ($r{=}\\val{sparsity.crossdoc_law.eval_r_code}$), so it\n  extrapolates \\emph{above} today's densest corpus; the honest density axis is\n  effective co-packed grants/pack, not raw out-degree. Text (wiki, arxiv) is flat or\n  slightly negative.\n  \\item \\textbf{\\cref{fig:sparsity-train} (train-time lines).} The same sweep but\n  \\emph{retraining} at each density, so the model learns under the sparsity it is\n  given. \\emph{Meaning:} the code curves are monotone but strongly concave --- most of\n  the benefit is captured by the first ${\\sim}25\\%$ of links (diminishing returns to\n  training density) --- and sit at or above the eval-time curve at low density.\n  \\item \\textbf{\\cref{fig:sparsity-grid} (2D train\\,$\\times$\\,eval surface).} The\n  headline result: the dominant gradient is \\emph{horizontal} (eval keep), not vertical\n  (train keep). \\emph{Meaning:} the cross-doc payoff is overwhelmingly an\n  \\emph{inference-time} capability --- a \\doccausal-trained code model (bottom row)\n  exploits cross-doc links at test time as well as, or better than, one trained with\n  them, so having linked content in context at inference matters far more than\n  training-time exposure. Wiki is the lone net-negative (blue) panel, and there\n  \\emph{training} on cross-doc links makes it worse while a \\doccausal wiki model is\n  barely