 first ${\\sim}25\\%$ of links (diminishing returns to\n  training density) --- and sit at or above the eval-time curve at low density.\n  \\item \\textbf{\\cref{fig:sparsity-grid} (2D train\\,$\\times$\\,eval surface).} The\n  headline result: the dominant gradient is \\emph{horizontal} (eval keep), not vertical\n  (train keep). \\emph{Meaning:} the cross-doc payoff is overwhelmingly an\n  \\emph{inference-time} capability --- a \\doccausal-trained code model (bottom row)\n  exploits cross-doc links at test time as well as, or better than, one trained with\n  them, so having linked content in context at inference matters far more than\n  training-time exposure. Wiki is the lone net-negative (blue) panel, and there\n  \\emph{training} on cross-doc links makes it worse while a \\doccausal wiki model is\n  barely affected.\n\\end{itemize}\n>>>>>>> origin/main\n=====05=====\nrather than replaying it.\n\n<<<<<<< HEAD\n\\paragraph{Recipe.}\nThe optimizer and schedule are inherited (\\Cref{app:recipe}) but two hyperparameters\nwere retuned for this data regime because the inherited values, set for a short\nFineWeb speedrun, were badly off: an eight-point Muon learning-rate sweep on the\n\\doccausal Wikipedia model found a clean U-curve with its minimum at\n$\\mu_\\text{lr}=0.003$ (the inherited $0.001$ was second-worst), and a weight-decay\nsweep found the inherited $1.2$ roughly an order of magnitude too high, with the\nminimum at $0.1$. AdamW's rate is coupled at $0.01\\times$ Muon's. Unless stated\notherwise every run uses $\\mu_\\text{lr}=0.003$, warmup $300$ steps, a linear cooldown\nover the final $40\\%$ to $0.1\\times$ peak, the embedding/head untie at two-thirds of\ntraining, and $\\texttt{max\\_grants}=256$. The weight decay of each arm is listed with\nits result (\\Cref{app:results}); within a paired contrast both arms share every\nhyperparameter, so the within-model $\\Delta$ is robust to the exact operating point.\n% Provenance for the sweeps: RESULTS.md \"LR RETUNE MILESTONE\" + \"Follow-on tuning\";\n% TUNING_LESSONS.md. The sweep U-curve values are training-log val losses and are not\n% yet ledger-grounded (see the log-source item in TODOS.md).\n=======\n\\todo{Benchmarks: HotpotQA-cross-doc (multi-hop, the headline wiki metric);\nRepoBench-cross-doc (Python/Java, headline code metric); community\\_pack held-out\ncross-doc perplexity; single-doc controls (HellaSwag, ARC, PIQA, WinoGrande,\nOpenBookQA, BoolQ, HumanEval).}\n\n\\paragraph{Contrasts and controls.}\nEach cross-document benchmark scores the same target tokens under two conditions:\n\\crossdoc attention with the granted linked document(s) present, versus the flat\n\\doccausal baseline over the identical context and completion (the cross-vs-flat\n$\\Delta$nll). Two further controls isolate the effect. The \\emph{placebo} (derangement)\nre-scores each fired query with the granted document's content replaced by a different\nexample's while holding the link identifiers fixed, so the grant still fires but the model\nattends to a wrong-but-plausible document; the placebo separation (placebo minus\ncross-document NLL) measures specificity to the correct link. To rule out verbatim\nre-exposure of duplicated text, we additionally stratify the $\\Delta$nll by the\ntarget--context $n$-gram overlap $\\alpha$ (RETRO's leakage protocol) and report the effect\nwithin the lowest-overlap stratum. Confidence intervals are 95\\% percentile bootstrap over\nthe paired per-example deltas.\n>>>>>>> origin/main\n\n\\paragraph{The cross-document contrast.}"},"type":"user","userType":"external","uuid":"1a12b8ff-9d62-48ec-81b6-524250ee8378","version":"2.1.252"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-09-01T17:18:33.251Z","schema":"annals/v1","stream":{"id":"claude-code:5cbbe416-90e8-4a21-93e7-044acf862880","seq":623}}
