{"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"diff --git a/paper/sections/05_experiments.tex b/paper/sections/05_experiments.tex\nindex d37d852..7aefbe7 100644\n--- a/paper/sections/05_experiments.tex\n+++ b/paper/sections/05_experiments.tex\n@@ -7,8 +7,21 @@\n \\todo{Benchmarks: HotpotQA-cross-doc (multi-hop, the headline wiki metric);\n RepoBench-cross-doc (Python/Java, headline code metric); community\\_pack held-out\n cross-doc perplexity; single-doc controls (HellaSwag, ARC, PIQA, WinoGrande,\n-OpenBookQA, BoolQ, HumanEval). Define the cross-doc-vs-flat-concat contrast and the\n-placebo control.}\n+OpenBookQA, BoolQ, HumanEval).}\n+\n+\\paragraph{Contrasts and controls.}\n+Each cross-document benchmark scores the same target tokens under two conditions:\n+\\crossdoc attention with the granted linked document(s) present, versus the flat\n+\\doccausal baseline over the identical context and completion (the cross-vs-flat\n+$\\Delta$nll). Two further controls isolate the effect. The \\emph{placebo} (derangement)\n+re-scores each fired query with the granted document's content replaced by a different\n+example's while holding the link identifiers fixed, so the grant still fires but the model\n+attends to a wrong-but-plausible document; the placebo separation (placebo minus\n+cross-document NLL) measures specificity to the correct link. To rule out verbatim\n+re-exposure of duplicated text, we additionally stratify the $\\Delta$nll by the\n+target--context $n$-gram overlap $\\alpha$ (RETRO's leakage protocol) and report the effect\n+within the lowest-overlap stratum. Confidence intervals are 95\\% percentile bootstrap over\n+the paired per-example deltas.\n \n \\todo{The 4-mask ablation and the traversal ablation (bfs/dfs/rw/random $\\times$\n dc/cdl) design.}\ndiff --git a/paper/sections/appendices/Z_additional_results.tex b/paper/sections/appendices/Z_additional_results.tex\nindex c895d9d..7bd8eab 100644\n--- a/paper/sections/appendices/Z_additional_results.tex\n+++ b/paper/sections/appendices/Z_additional_results.tex\n@@ -2,3 +2,46 @@\n \\label{app:results}\n \\todo{Full per-language sweep tables, per-traversal ablation tables, community\\_pack\n 32k re-run numbers, hyperparameter sweep (LR/WD U-curves).}\n+\n+\\subsection{Graph-sparsity: cross-dataset regression detail}\n+\\label{app:sparsity-regression}\n+\\begin{figure}[t]\n+  \\centering\n+  \\includegraphics[width=0.72\\linewidth]{regression.png}\n+  \\caption{Pooled cross-dataset regression of cross-doc benefit vs effective grant\n+  density $\\times$ keep fraction, train-time (blue) vs eval-time (orange), code\n+  (circles) vs text (crosses), with code OLS fits.}\n+  \\label{fig:sparsity-regression}\n+\\end{figure}\n+\\begin{itemize}\n+  \\item \\textbf{\\cref{fig:sparsity-regression}.} The quantitative form of the\n+  cross-dataset law in \\cref{sec:sparsity}, pooling all (dataset, keep) points on the\n+  honest density axis (effective grants/pack $\\times$ keep). \\emph{Meaning:} code is\n+  near-linear both eval-time ($r{=}0.95$) and train-time ($r{=}0.84$); the lower\n+  train-time $r$ and its positive intercept ($\\sim{+}0.016$) reflect the concavity\n+  (much of the benefit already present at low density). Text datasets sit off the code\n+  line. Raw graph out-degree is the wrong axis ($r{=}{-}0.27$, a misleading null)\n+  because it counts edges the mask never used.\n+\\end{itemize}\n+\n+\\subsection{Graph-sparsity: mask-time vs traversal-time validation}\n+\\label{app:sparsity-traversal}\n+\\begin{figure}[t]\n+  \\centering\n+  \\includegraphics[width=0.62\\linewidth]{fig_traversal_check.png}\n+  \\caption{Train-time $\\Delta$nll vs effective grant density for \\textsc{typescript}:\n+  cheap \\emph{mask-time} dropping (blue, packing fixed) vs \\emph{traversal-time}\n+  dropping (orange, graph adjacency thinned before packing so co-packing itself\n+  changes --- a genuinely sparser corpus). Error bars are 95\\% CIs.}\n+  \\label{fig:sparsity-traversal}\n+\\end{figure}\n+\\begin{itemize}\n+  \\item \\textbf{\\cref{fig:sparsity-traversal}.} A validity check on the instrument\n+  behind every density line: does the cheap mask-time subsample (thin recorded grants\n+  on identical packs) predict the effect of a \\emph{really} sparser corpus, where fewer\n+  links also reshuffle which documents are co-packed? \\emph{Meaning:} the traversal-time\n+  points fall on the mask-time dose-response curve at matched effective density (both\n+  within CI), so the content-distribution shift from real edge removal does not move the\n+  curve --- density drives the benefit, and mask-time dropping is a faithful, far\n+  cheaper proxy.\n+\\end{itemize}\n=== ledger additions ===\n+++ b/provenance/ledger.yaml\n+\n+  # === Placebo (derangement) control + leakage-α stratification on the headline arms ===\n+  # Tier-2 harness (eval/benchmark_harness) run on the SAME single-source cross_doc_link\n+  # checkpoints that back the grounded headline cross-doc numbers above.\n+  #   placebo_sep = mean(placebo_nll - cross_nll): grants kept firing on the ORIGINAL\n+  #     identifiers but attention reads a deranged (wrong) donor doc; CI excluding 0 proves\n+  #     the gain needs the RIGHT document, not just extra in-language context.\n+  #   leakage_lowalpha.delta_nll = flat-cross Δnll restricted to the lowest target<->aux\n+  #     8-gram overlap stratum (α<0.05); CI excluding 0 rules out verbatim re-exposure\n+  #     (RETRO bpb(α) protocol). Sidecar: runs/<id>/eval_leakage_placebo.json.\n+\n+  # Python RepoBench (thestack cross_doc_link, max_grants=256)\n+  crossdoc.repobench_python.placebo_sep:\n+    run_ids: [run_20260720_063128_690228]\n+    source: {kind: eval, metric_path: \"repobench_python_xdoc_placebo/native\", field: placebo_separation}\n+    expected: 0.1295747930227324\n+    units: nll\n+    note: \"Python RepoBench placebo separation (placebo - cross mean NLL).\"\n+  crossdoc.repobench_python.placebo_sep.ci:\n+    run_ids: [run_20260720_063128_690228]\n+    source: {kind: eval, metric_path: \"repobench_python_xdoc_placebo/native\", field: placebo_separation_ci}\n+    format: {kind: interval, sig_figs: 3}\n+    note: \"Python RepoBench placebo separation 95% bootstrap CI.\"\n+  crossdoc.repobench_python.leakage_lowalpha.delta_nll:\n+    run_ids: [run_20260720_063128_690228]\n+    source: {kind: eval, metric_path: \"repobench_python_leakage/alpha_0.00_0.05\", field: delta_real}\n+    expected: 0.07807492273640258\n+    units: nll\n+    note: \"Python RepoBench cross-doc Δnll in the lowest overlap stratum (α<0.05).\"\n+  crossdoc.repobench_python.leakage_lowalpha.delta_nll.ci:\n+    run_ids: [run_20260720_063128_690228]\n+    source: {kind: eval, metric_path: \"repobench_python_leakage/alpha_0.00_0.05\", field: delta_real_ci}\n+    format: {kind: interval, sig_figs: 3}\n+    note: \"95% bootstrap CI for the α<0.05 Python stratum Δnll.\"\n+  crossdoc.repobench_python.leakage_lowalpha.n:\n+    run_ids: [run_20260720_063128_690228]\n+    source: {kind: eval, metric_path: \"repobench_python_leakage/alpha_0.00_0.05\", field: n}\n+    expected: 191\n+    format: {sig_figs: 0}\n+    note: \"Number of scored pairs in the α<0.05 stratum (Python).\"\n+\n+  # Java RepoBench (thestack cross_doc_link, bfs traversal)\n+  crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.placebo_sep:\n+    run_ids: [run_20260722_063905_465684]\n+    source: {kind: eval, metric_path: \"repobench_java_xdoc_placebo/native\", field: placebo_separation}\n+    expected: 0.07738205790519714\n+    units: nll\n+    note: \"Java RepoBench (bfs) placebo separation.\"\n+  crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.placebo_sep.ci:\n+    run_ids: [run_20260722_063905_465684]\n+    source: {kind: eval, metric_path: \"repobench_java_xdoc_placebo/native\", field: placebo_separation_ci}\n+    format: {kind: interval, sig_figs: 3}\n+    note: \"Java RepoBench (bfs) placebo separation 95% bootstrap CI.\"\n+  crossdoc.repobench_java.bfs.leakage_lowalpha.delta_nll:\n+    run_ids: [run_20260722_063905_4656